{"id": 613, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 62-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a generalized tonic-clonic seizure.\u00a0 His wife reports that he has been having episodic headaches, nausea, and progressive weakness for the past several weeks.\u00a0 The headaches are particularly worse at night and occasionally awaken him.\u00a0 Other medical conditions include well-controlled hypertension and osteoarthritis.\u00a0 On physical examination, the patient is postictal but follows simple commands.\u00a0 He is admitted to the hospital for further evaluation but dies several days later due to aspiration pneumonia and septic shock.\u00a0 During autopsy, examination of the brain shows a poorly circumscribed, hemorrhagic, necrotic mass in the left cerebral hemisphere with a midline shift away from the mass.\u00a0 Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Brain metastasis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Glioblastoma"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Meningioma"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oligodendroglioma"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Primary brain lymphoma"}, {"label": "F", "text": "Schwannoma"}], "correctAnswer": "B", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_T91170\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">P</mark>athology of common adult primary brain tumors</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Macroscopic appearance</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Microscopic appearance</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Metastasesto the brain</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Multiple lesions at gray-white matter junction</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Well circumscribed</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Often resembles primary tumor</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Meningioma</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Extraaxial</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Well circumscribed</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Dural attachment</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Oval cells forming whorled pattern</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Laminated calcifications (psammoma bodies)</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Glioblastoma</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Cerebral hemispheres</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Poorly circumscribed</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Necrotic &amp; hemorrhagic</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Malignant astrocytes</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Microvascular proliferation</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Pseudopalisading necrosis</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Oligodendroglioma</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Often in frontal lobe</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Calcified</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Cells with round nuclei &amp; clear perinuclear halo (\"fried egg\")</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">\"Chicken-wire\" capillaries</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Schwannoma</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Often at cerebellopontine angle</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Encapsulated</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Attached to nerve</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Spindle-shaped Schwann cells</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Hypercellular (Antoni A) &amp; hypocellular (Antoni B) areas</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient's brain mass likely represents <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">glioblastoma</strong>, the most common primary cerebral tumor in adults.\u00a0 Glioblastoma is an astrocytic tumor typically located within the <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-pru7btptogqt98jan70lxx38\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-pru7btptogqt98jan70lxx38\">cerebral hemispheres</a>.\u00a0 These tumors produce high amounts of vascular endothelial growth factor, resulting in rapid tumor growth and substantial microvascular proliferation.\u00a0 The newly formed (highly fenestrated) vessels generate large amounts of vasogenic edema, causing significant mass effect (frequently with midline shift).\u00a0 Patients have symptoms of increased intracranial pressure (eg, headache, nausea), focal neurologic deficits (eg, weakness), and seizures that <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">rapidly progress</strong> within weeks.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">On gross examination, glioblastomas are typically <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">poorly circumscribed</strong> lesions with a variegated appearance, often showing areas of <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">necrosis</strong> and <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">hemorrhage</strong>.\u00a0 Some may cross the corpus callosum, creating a characteristic <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-ahg2w8g1d7f7b35ct8pg41po\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-ahg2w8g1d7f7b35ct8pg41po\">butterfly shape</a>.\u00a0 Glioblastomas are highly malignant with a very poor prognosis; most patients die within 2 years of diagnosis.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Most adult brain tumors represent metastases from other cancers (eg, lung, breast, melanoma).\u00a0 However, on gross examination, brain metastases typically appear as multiple, well-circumscribed masses at the junction of the gray and white matter.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice C)</strong>\u00a0 Meningiomas are benign, slow-growing tumors of adults and are derived from meningothelial cells of the arachnoid (external to the brain parenchyma).\u00a0 They are typically located at sites of dural reflection (eg, falx cerebri, tentorium cerebelli), and gross examination often shows a well-circumscribed mass attached to the dura.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 Oligodendrogliomas are slow-growing tumors in adults that typically involve the white matter of the cerebral hemispheres, often the frontal lobe.\u00a0 On gross examination, they usually appear as gray masses with calcification.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Primary CNS lymphoma is often seen in immunocompromised individuals (eg, patients with AIDS, transplant recipients).\u00a0 Gross examination usually shows solitary or multiple lesions that involve the deep periventricular white matter.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice F)</strong>\u00a0 Schwannomas are benign tumors that commonly arise from the vestibular branch of the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) at the cerebellopontine angle (vestibular schwannoma).\u00a0 Patients typically have sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus.\u00a0 Gross examination often shows an encapsulated mass attached to the nerve.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Glioblastoma is the most common primary cerebral tumor of adults and is typically located within the cerebral hemispheres, occasionally crossing the midline (butterfly glioma).\u00a0 Gross examination of this highly malignant tumor often shows a poorly circumscribed, variegated mass containing areas of necrosis and hemorrhage.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Glioblastoma is the most common primary cerebral tumor of adults and is typically located within the cerebral hemispheres, occasionally crossing the midline (butterfly glioma).  Gross examination of this highly malignant tumor often shows a poorly circumscribed, variegated mass containing areas of necrosis and hemorrhage.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\